alfe-bricscad-batch-hidden-ui — pass /Automation with /b on Windows to run BricsCAD batch with its main frame hidden
===================================================================================================================

Affected:
  autolisp-front-end/source/backend-bricscad.lisp
  autolisp-front-end/tests/backend-cad-tests.lisp

Context
-------

The Windows BricsCAD batch backend currently launches:

  bricscad.exe <template> [/p <profile>] /b <workdir>/run.scr

`build-launch-argv` deliberately uses the direct executable + `/b`
path because it is simpler and more reliable than the legacy
COM/VBScript bridge. The direct path currently opens BricsCAD's main
window, even though ALFE only needs the script-driven protocol.

Bricsys documents that the Windows-only `/Automation` startup switch:

  - starts BricsCAD without displaying the main frame window;
  - was intended for COM automation;
  - is also useful for batch processing that does not use COM;
  - can be combined directly with `/B`;
  - requires the loaded script to close BricsCAD when processing ends.

The documented example is:

  <BricsCAD.exe> /automation /B <scriptFileName.scr>

Source, checked 2026-07-31:
  https://help.bricsys.com/en-us/document/bricscad/customization/startup-options

The switch therefore does not require ALFE to use COM. It is an
independent startup option that can hide the UI while the existing
`run.scr` file protocol drives BricsCAD.

Terminology warning
-------------------

ALFE already calls the legacy COM/VBScript backend `:automation` and
exposes it as `--mode automation`. BricsCAD also names its executable
switch `/Automation`. These are not the same mechanism:

  - ALFE `:batch` on Windows: direct `bricscad.exe ... /b run.scr`;
  - proposed change: direct `bricscad.exe /Automation ... /b run.scr`;
  - ALFE `:automation`: `cscript bridge-bricscad.vbs`, using COM and
    `SendCommand`.

This issue changes only the direct Windows batch argv. It does not
replace, rename, or otherwise modify the legacy COM backend.

Required change
---------------

In `build-launch-argv`, add `/Automation` to the direct batch command
when `windows-p` is true. Do not pass it on macOS or Linux, where the
switch is not supported.

The effective Windows command should be equivalent to:

  bricscad.exe /Automation <template> [/p <profile>] /b <workdir>/run.scr

The exact argument position may follow the existing argv construction,
provided `/Automation` precedes the `/b` script pair and BricsCAD V25
accepts the resulting command.

`run.scr` already issues `_FILEDIA 0`, loads `run-common.lsp`, and
closes BricsCAD on normal completion. Preserve those semantics.

Why this matters for CI
-----------------------

SCHMS+ is preparing a Windows GitLab Runner host. BricsCAD is normally
invoked once per ALFE invocation and exits after that invocation. A
single CI job may perform several successive ALFE/BricsCAD cycles.

Hiding the main frame is valuable in both contemplated deployments:

  1. an interactive runner or `alfe-back` in a persistent RDP session;
  2. a GitLab Runner Windows service with no interactive session, if
     BricsCAD can initialize successfully in session 0.

`/Automation` removes the visible main frame but does not by itself
prove that BricsCAD can run on a noninteractive Windows window station.
Session-0 support remains an empirical integration test, not an
assumption of this change.

Tests
-----

Unit tests, runnable without BricsCAD:

  - add or extend a `build-launch-argv` test for a simulated Windows
    host;
  - assert that the direct Windows batch argv contains exactly one
    `/Automation` argument;
  - assert that `/Automation` appears before `/b` and its script path;
  - assert that the Windows command still contains the configured
    template and `/p` profile when present;
  - assert that the macOS/Linux batch argv does not contain
    `/Automation` and still uses `-B`;
  - assert that explicit ALFE `:automation` mode still launches
    `cscript`/VBScript on Windows.

Windows integration tests, requiring BricsCAD V25:

  1. Run a minimal `alfe --bricscad --mode batch -x ...` invocation in
     an ordinary interactive session. It reaches READY, executes the
     request, writes DONE, and exits without displaying the main frame.
  2. Run the same command in a disconnected RDP session.
  3. Run it from GitLab Runner installed as a service after reboot,
     with no user session open. Record whether BricsCAD reaches READY;
     failure here does not invalidate the argv change, but determines
     whether CI needs the RDP/interactive-agent architecture.
  4. Verify that abnormal ALFE termination still kills BricsCAD and
     releases the network licence.

Documentation synchronisation
-----------------------------

Update all documentation tracks affected by the launch command:

  - source comments at the top of `backend-bricscad.lisp`;
  - function docstrings/comments describing Windows batch mode;
  - ALFE user manual and specifications where the BricsCAD launch
    command or batch/automation distinction is documented;
  - `alfe --help` and its man page only if their user-visible mode
    description needs clarification.

Make it explicit that `/Automation` is a BricsCAD executable switch in
the direct batch backend and does not mean that ALFE selected its COM
`--mode automation` backend.

Acceptance criteria
-------------------

  - On Windows, direct BricsCAD batch argv is
    `bricscad.exe ... /Automation ... /b run.scr`.
  - On non-Windows platforms, direct batch argv is unchanged.
  - The COM/VBScript backend remains available and unchanged through
    explicit ALFE `--mode automation`.
  - Unit tests pin the platform-specific argv and the distinction
    between direct hidden-UI batch and COM automation.
  - A BricsCAD V25 Windows integration run reaches READY, executes an
    ALFE request, reports DONE, and exits cleanly.
  - The result of the no-session/service experiment is recorded.
  - Synchronized documentation accurately describes the new command.
  - The ALFE version is bumped according to project convention.

